{
  "teamId": 133,
  "year": 2024,
  "narrative": "The 2024 Oakland Athletics played their final season in Oakland as a transitional team in every sense — young, volatile, and ultimately outgunned, but not without their moments of genuine excitement. They opened the year with a modest winning record in March and April, powered by explosive performances from catcher Shea Langeliers and emerging infielder Zack Gelof, giving fans in the Coliseum something to cheer about in the twilight of an era.\n\nMay brought a Jekyll-and-Hyde stretch that encapsulated the whole season. The A's went just 9-19, yet the highest-leverage moments swung wildly in both directions — clutch home runs from Kyle McCann and Miguel Andujar alternating with gut-punch surrendered homers and bullpen implosions. June was the nadir, a 7-20 month in which the pitching staff gave up crucial long balls and the offense couldn't compensate, sending Oakland spiraling toward the bottom of the AL West.\n\nJuly offered a surprising reprieve — a 15-9 record that stands as the season's lone extended bright spot, though the play log is thin in that stretch, suggesting the wins came quietly and the losses came loudly. August was a coin-flip month at 14-12, but September returned the team to losing ways, and the final weeks were marked by a string of heartbreaking home runs surrendered, including a devastating three-run blast allowed by Mason Miller on September 1st that swung the win probability nearly a full run in the opponent's favor.\n\nThe season ended on a bittersweet grace note: Shea Langeliers — the face of Oakland's future wherever that future leads — crushed his 29th home run on the final day of the season in a moment that felt like both a farewell and a promise. At 69-93, the A's missed the playoffs comfortably, but Langeliers, Gelof, and Miller gave the faithful a glimpse of what the next chapter might hold in Sacramento, and eventually in Las Vegas.",
  "chapters": [
